WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.430
<v Speaker 1>I think it is a terrifying thought for anyone who spends their life learning, honestly.

2
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:05.390
<v Speaker 2>Oh, absolutely.

3
00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:19.390
<v Speaker 1>Just the idea that you could dedicate decades to gathering facts and mastering all these complex arguments, building this huge intellect, only to find out that the sheer volume of your learning is exactly the thing that ruins you.

4
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.270
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it is a devastating reality to confront.

5
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:22.990
<v Speaker 1>It really is.

6
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:23.230
<v Speaker 2>Yeah.

7
00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:29.470
<v Speaker 1>And we are looking directly at you, the listener, as we open the final pages of the text called O Beloved Son.

8
00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:33.790
<v Speaker 1>This is just such a deeply historical, urgent correspondence.

9
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.150
<v Speaker 1>It is a master writing back to his student.

10
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:40.430
<v Speaker 1>And this student had desperately asked for a functional path, not just more theory.

11
00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:40.830
<v Speaker 2>Right.

12
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:42.670
<v Speaker 2>He wanted something he could actually apply.

13
00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:43.390
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

14
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.110
<v Speaker 1>So for this deep dive, our mission is to examine the master's ultimate framework.

15
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:56.750
<v Speaker 1>At the very end of his letter, he distills decades of spiritual and intellectual observation, and he hands the disciple 8 distinct contours.

16
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.030
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, the final instructions.

17
00:00:58.270 --> 00:00:58.670
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

18
00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:07.630
<v Speaker 1>He explicitly outlines 4 habits that the seeker must categorically refuse and four quiet habits that must be actively taken up.

19
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:14.270
<v Speaker 1>And the purpose of this framework is singular, so that his hard-earned knowledge does not turn against him on the day of judgment.

20
00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:23.150
<v Speaker 2>Of these eight admonitions, there are four things you must do and four things you must not do so that your knowledge does not turn against you on the day of judgment.

21
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:28.030
<v Speaker 1>That single sentence, I mean, it establishes an incredibly high stakes reality.

22
00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:28.670
<v Speaker 2>It does.

23
00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.390
<v Speaker 1>We spend decades sitting in classrooms, reading texts, absorbing information.

24
00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:39.310
<v Speaker 1>We operate under this assumption that knowing the truth is the exact same thing as being transformed by it.

25
00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:40.830
<v Speaker 2>Which is a massive illusion.

26
00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.190
<v Speaker 1>It is, and the master is stripping that illusion away completely.

27
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:52.270
<v Speaker 1>He is telling the disciple that having the right information or being able to articulate the correct theological points decides absolutely nothing about his ultimate fate.

28
00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.230
<v Speaker 2>Nothing at all.

29
00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:57.950
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge is entirely inert until it takes physical and psychological form in our actions.

30
00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:02.310
<v Speaker 2>What that knowledge becomes when it is translated into conduct is literally.

31
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:04.830
<v Speaker 1>The only one.

32
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.830
<v Speaker 2>And the master, Gaz A.H.

33
00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:13.550
<v Speaker 2>Lee, structures these final instructions in a way that reveals just a deep understanding of human psychology.

34
00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.190
<v Speaker 2>He begins with the four prohibitions, the things the disciple must completely refrain from.

35
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.830
<v Speaker 1>And what is so unsettling about these four traps

36
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.830
<v Speaker 1>is that they do not look like traps.

37
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:23.670
<v Speaker 2>No, not at all.

38
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.310
<v Speaker 1>They are not outward crimes.

39
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:30.110
<v Speaker 1>They are subtle, they are intellectual, and they are highly respected within religious circles.

40
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.390
<v Speaker 2>That is the danger.

41
00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:37.710
<v Speaker 2>A person slides into them precisely because the religious surface of the act looks completely intact.

42
00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.910
<v Speaker 1>I mean, if a trap looks like a trap, you walk around it.

43
00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:40.510
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

44
00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:46.270
<v Speaker 1>But if a trap looks like a noble pursuit of truth, you walk right into it, completely convinced of your own righteousness.

45
00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:46.910
<v Speaker 2>You do.

46
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.870
<v Speaker 1>So let us start with the first admonition, because it is bracing and it's

47
00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:51.630
<v Speaker 1>absolute sweep.

48
00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:56.750
<v Speaker 1>He instructs the disciple to categorically refuse the habit of religious debate.

49
00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.030
<v Speaker 2>Completely refuse it.

50
00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:05.390
<v Speaker 1>He tells him to avoid, as far as possible, engaging in disputes with anyone, on any matter, any matter at all.

51
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.030
<v Speaker 2>Not just trivial things, everything.

52
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:12.590
<v Speaker 1>I have to admit, my immediate reaction to that sweeping prohibition is to push back.

53
00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:13.550
<v Speaker 2>Oh, for sure.

54
00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:23.310
<v Speaker 1>I mean, if a person possesses knowledge and they see someone spreading falsehood or misunderstanding, isn't there a moral obligation to step in and defend the truth?

55
00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.230
<v Speaker 2>That is what the ego wants you to think.

56
00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.190
<v Speaker 1>Well, yeah.

57
00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.510
<v Speaker 1>How can walking away from an argument be the righteous choice

58
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.350
<v Speaker 1>when the truth is under attack.

59
00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.110
<v Speaker 2>It feels like an abdication of duty.

60
00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.070
<v Speaker 1>It does.

61
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:42.510
<v Speaker 2>The ego desperately wants to frame it as this noble defense of the sacred, but the master is looking past that surface excuse.

62
00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:47.470
<v Speaker 2>He is pointing directly to the hidden internal harms that these disputes generate.

63
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.910
<v Speaker 1>The psychological damage.

64
00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:49.710
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

65
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:57.390
<v Speaker 2>He's stating that the psychological damage inflicted upon your own soul during a debate far outweighs any theoretical benefit of winning the argument.

66
00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.110
<v Speaker 1>He actually lists the harvest of this habit, doesn't he?

67
00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:00.590
<v Speaker 2>Does.

68
00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:07.070
<v Speaker 2>He lists the observable harvest that the habit of disputation produces, and it is a catastrophic list for the soul.

69
00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:13.870
<v Speaker 2>He says it produces ostentation, jealousy, arrogance, resentment, enmity, pride, and egotism.

70
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.950
<v Speaker 1>Those are the exact traits that ruin the heart.

71
00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:17.310
<v Speaker 2>Completely ruin it.

72
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.790
<v Speaker 1>But I really want to understand the mechanism here.

73
00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:26.350
<v Speaker 1>Why does arguing about a point of objective truth inherently produce arrogance or resentment?

74
00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.990
<v Speaker 1>What is actually happening inside the person?

75
00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:36.430
<v Speaker 2>Well, the profound psychological insight the master offers is that the human soul is just an unreliable narrator of its own motives.

76
00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:37.550
<v Speaker 1>Unreliable how?

77
00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:50.030
<v Speaker 2>In the heat of an argument, when your blood is rushing and another person is publicly contradicting your intellect, the soul cannot distinguish between defending the objective truth and defending its own fragile ego.

78
00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:51.550
<v Speaker 1>They blend together.

79
00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.030
<v Speaker 2>They do.

80
00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:57.870
<v Speaker 2>You think you are fighting for the purity of the doctrine, but you are actually just fighting to assert your own supremacy.

81
00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.110
<v Speaker 2>You are fighting not to be humiliated.

82
00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.390
<v Speaker 1>I mean, that makes perfect sense.

83
00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:06.190
<v Speaker 1>The soul wraps its desire for dominance in the noble cloak of defending the faith.

84
00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:06.950
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

85
00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:11.870
<v Speaker 1>When someone contradicts a point I have made, the immediate physical sensation is defensive.

86
00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:13.230
<v Speaker 1>It is a sting.

87
00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:14.670
<v Speaker 1>I want to prove them wrong.

88
00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:18.670
<v Speaker 1>Not just so the truth is known, but so that I am recognized as the one who knew it.

89
00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.990
<v Speaker 2>And that is the ostentation he warns about.

90
00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:23.470
<v Speaker 2>When you debate, you are performing.

91
00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:24.790
<v Speaker 1>You are on stage.

92
00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:31.470
<v Speaker 2>You want the opponent and any onlookers to recognize how articulate, clever, and well-read you are.

93
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.830
<v Speaker 2>The performance becomes entirely detached from the sincere desire to serve the creator.

94
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.230
<v Speaker 1>And the outcomes are lose-lose for the soul.

95
00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:40.430
<v Speaker 2>They really are.

96
00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:44.430
<v Speaker 2>If your opponent makes a better point, it breeds resentment and jealousy.

97
00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:44.830
<v Speaker 2>If you

98
00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:47.550
<v Speaker 2>To make a better point, it breeds arrogance and pride.

99
00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.270
<v Speaker 2>The ego feeds regardless of the outcome, while the heart starves.

100
00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.190
<v Speaker 1>That is a terrifying bind to be in.

101
00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:59.310
<v Speaker 1>But the master does not say debate is universally forbidden in every conceivable instance, does he?

102
00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.070
<v Speaker 2>No, he does provide an exception.

103
00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:02.990
<v Speaker 1>A sort of internal diagnostic test.

104
00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.710
<v Speaker 2>Yes, but the bar to clear is incredibly high.

105
00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:13.550
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple that if an argument arises and your genuine singular desire is simply for the truth to prevail, then your intention might be sound.

106
00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:14.310
<v Speaker 1>Might be sound.

107
00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:14.750
<v Speaker 2>Right.

108
00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:20.270
<v Speaker 2>However, knowing how deceptive the ego is, you cannot simply declare to yourself that your intention is pure.

109
00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:24.910
<v Speaker 2>You have to rigorously test that intention against two specific inward signs.

110
00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:30.510
<v Speaker 1>Let us break down those two signs, because they seem designed to completely short circuit the ego.

111
00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.030
<v Speaker 1>What is the first sign?

112
00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:37.710
<v Speaker 2>The first sign is total absolute indifference to whose mouth the truth comes from.

113
00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:38.670
<v Speaker 1>Total indifference.

114
00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:46.350
<v Speaker 2>Whether the truth ultimately emerges from your own tongue or from the tongue of your opponent, it must make absolutely no difference to your emotional state.

115
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:49.230
<v Speaker 2>You must be equally satisfied in either case.

116
00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:55.190
<v Speaker 1>I want to linger on that because achieving that state requires a massive dismantling of the self.

117
00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:55.870
<v Speaker 2>It really does.

118
00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.030
<v Speaker 1>If I am in a tense discussion,

119
00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:05.630
<v Speaker 1>and the other person suddenly articulates the perfect resolving point, my initial reaction is almost never pure joy.

120
00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.070
<v Speaker 2>No, rarely.

121
00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:11.630
<v Speaker 1>It is usually a very quiet internal frustration that I didn't think of at first.

122
00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:14.150
<v Speaker 1>I feel defeated even if the issue is resolved.

123
00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:19.550
<v Speaker 2>And the master is saying that if you feel even a fraction of that sting, your intention was corrupt from the start.

124
00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:20.670
<v Speaker 1>That is a heavy standard.

125
00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:23.550
<v Speaker 2>If you feel that sting, you are defending yourself.

126
00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:24.990
<v Speaker 2>You are not defending the truth.

127
00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:31.270
<v Speaker 2>If you were truly a servant of the truth, you would rejoice that the truth arrived, regardless of which vessel carried it.

128
00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:34.110
<v Speaker 1>Because the ego demands to be the hero of the story.

129
00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.870
<v Speaker 2>Yes, but the sincere heart just wants the story to end with the truth.

130
00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:41.470
<v Speaker 1>That alone probably disqualifies 99% of the debates we engage in.

131
00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:42.030
<v Speaker 2>Easily.

132
00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.310
<v Speaker 1>What is the second sign?

133
00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:50.910
<v Speaker 2>The second sign is that you actively consider it preferable to discuss the matter in private, away from any audience or spectators.

134
00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.350
<v Speaker 1>A preference for isolation.

135
00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:56.030
<v Speaker 1>Why is the absence of an audience so critical?

136
00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:01.070
<v Speaker 2>Because spectators are the absolute fuel for ostentation.

137
00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:02.030
<v Speaker 1>They invite performance.

138
00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:02.910
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

139
00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:08.750
<v Speaker 2>When others are watching, the scakes of the argument shift from what is right to who looks better.

140
00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:16.190
<v Speaker 2>If you truly solely care about resolving A misunderstanding with another human being, you do not need anyone else in the room.

141
00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:17.550
<v Speaker 1>You just need the other person.

142
00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:18.110
<v Speaker 2>Right.

143
00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:27.790
<v Speaker 2>If you find yourself wanting others to hear the debate, wanting them to witness your cleverness or see you dismantle the other person's logic, then your heart has already abandoned the truth.

144
00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:29.870
<v Speaker 1>It is busy building a monument to itself.

145
00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:30.910
<v Speaker 2>Beautifully put.

146
00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:38.270
<v Speaker 2>And both of these signs, the indifference to the source of truth and the preference for privacy are entirely inward.

147
00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:39.110
<v Speaker 2>Only you

148
00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:41.150
<v Speaker 2>You and your creator know if you possess them.

149
00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:46.590
<v Speaker 1>This framework completely changes how a person of knowledge should interact with questions and challenges.

150
00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:52.110
<v Speaker 1>It demands a level of self-interrogation that is exhausting but clearly necessary.

151
00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:53.710
<v Speaker 2>It is necessary for survival.

152
00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:57.950
<v Speaker 1>And this leads directly into a fascinating aside the master provides.

153
00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:01.390
<v Speaker 1>He recognizes that a scholar is going to face questions.

154
00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:03.870
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it is inevitable.

155
00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:10.910
<v Speaker 1>So he offers a practical typology for handling questioners using this extended analogy of a physician and a patient.

156
00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:12.430
<v Speaker 2>It is a vital framework.

157
00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:19.070
<v Speaker 2>Dealing with questions that originate from a corrupt place is one of the most draining aspects of a life dedicated to study.

158
00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:20.270
<v Speaker 1>So what is the analogy?

159
00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:29.310
<v Speaker 2>The master establishes that asking questions about complex religious and spiritual matters is like describing the invisible diseases of the heart to a physician.

160
00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.590
<v Speaker 2>The ignorant questioners are the patients.

161
00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:34.510
<v Speaker 2>the scholars are the physicians of the heart.

162
00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:38.670
<v Speaker 1>But he makes a very sharp distinction about the qualifications of the physician too.

163
00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:43.150
<v Speaker 1>He doesn't just assume anyone who has read a book can cure these diseases.

164
00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:45.070
<v Speaker 2>He is very strict about this.

165
00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:55.710
<v Speaker 2>He notes that imperfect scholars, those who have gathered facts but have not purified their own intentions, are utterly incapable of curing these deep spiritual diseases.

166
00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:58.230
<v Speaker 1>They cannot heal what they suffer from themselves.

167
00:09:58.230 --> 00:09:58.830
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

168
00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.830
<v Speaker 2>Only A perfected scholar can cure them.

169
00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:01.590
<v Speaker 2>More

170
00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:07.230
<v Speaker 2>Actually, only a perfected scholar has the diagnostic vision to identify the root causes of the disease.

171
00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:15.790
<v Speaker 2>He warns that if a disease worsens because its true causes cannot be identified, the patient must seek a more expert physician.

172
00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:21.230
<v Speaker 2>A true physician of the heart knows exactly which ailments are curable and which are terminal.

173
00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:26.670
<v Speaker 1>And expending effort trying to cure an incurable disease is, in his words, nothing but a waste of time.

174
00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:27.630
<v Speaker 2>A complete waste.

175
00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:32.270
<v Speaker 1>So he breaks down these patients, these various types of questioners into four specific categories.

176
00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:36.830
<v Speaker 1>And he makes the startling claim that only one of them is actually worth the medicine.

177
00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.190
<v Speaker 2>Yes, only one.

178
00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.750
<v Speaker 1>Let us examine the incurable cases first.

179
00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:42.670
<v Speaker 1>Who is the first patient?

180
00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:44.630
<v Speaker 2>The first patient is the jealous one.

181
00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:45.390
<v Speaker 1>The jealous one.

182
00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:53.110
<v Speaker 2>This is the questioner who asks not to learn, but out of hidden jealousy or who raises endless objections purely from envy.

183
00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:55.310
<v Speaker 1>And what is the master's diagnosis here?

184
00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:56.990
<v Speaker 2>It is uncompromising.

185
00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:01.630
<v Speaker 2>He states that jealousy is a lethal, chronic disease that has no cure.

186
00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:02.990
<v Speaker 1>Wait, no cure at all.

187
00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:06.430
<v Speaker 1>That is a definitive verdict to render on another human being.

188
00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:07.070
<v Speaker 2>It is.

189
00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:11.150
<v Speaker 1>Why is jealousy categorized as entirely incurable by the scholar?

190
00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:14.990
<v Speaker 2>Because the nature of jealousy makes the very act of teaching counterproductive.

191
00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:15.470
<v Speaker 1>How so?

192
00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:18.910
<v Speaker 2>It is a condition that consumes the reality of the afflicted person.

193
00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:27.710
<v Speaker 2>The master explains to the disciple that whatever answer you give to the jealous one, no matter how profoundly sound, articulate, or compassionate it is,

194
00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.590
<v Speaker 2>That person will only regard you as an enemy.

195
00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:31.870
<v Speaker 1>Because they don't want an answer.

196
00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:32.510
<v Speaker 2>Right.

197
00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:37.950
<v Speaker 2>The fire of their envy will only burn more fiercely with every true and beautiful word you speak.

198
00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:38.830
<v Speaker 1>That is tragic.

199
00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:45.670
<v Speaker 1>Because the truth and eloquence of your words only serve to highlight the exact qualities they feel they lack.

200
00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:46.430
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

201
00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:50.430
<v Speaker 1>By answering them brilliantly, you are actually worsening their disease.

202
00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:52.830
<v Speaker 2>Your excellence is the source of their pain.

203
00:11:53.630 --> 00:12:02.110
<v Speaker 2>He cites wisdom stating that every kind of animosity has the potential to be remedied except the animosity that is born of jealousy.

204
00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:03.630
<v Speaker 1>It cannot be negotiated with.

205
00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:04.430
<v Speaker 2>It cannot.

206
00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:08.190
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, he instructs the disciple to simply turn away.

207
00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.710
<v Speaker 2>Leave the jealous person to remain afflicted with their disease.

208
00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:12.270
<v Speaker 1>Walk away.

209
00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:12.830
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

210
00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:18.990
<v Speaker 2>There is absolutely no intellectual answer you can provide that will help them because their problem is not intellectual.

211
00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:21.630
<v Speaker 2>It is a profound spiritual resentment.

212
00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:23.870
<v Speaker 1>Your engagement only feeds their sickness.

213
00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:29.150
<v Speaker 1>Turning away from someone who is actively objecting to your work takes an immense amount of discipline.

214
00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:32.830
<v Speaker 1>The natural instinct is to defend yourself, to prove them wrong.

215
00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:41.150
<v Speaker 1>But recognizing that their malice is a symptom of an incurable disease and just letting it go requires real spiritual maturity.

216
00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:42.830
<v Speaker 2>It is the only safe response.

217
00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:45.390
<v Speaker 1>Then we move to the second category of patient.

218
00:12:45.550 --> 00:12:47.390
<v Speaker 2>The second patient is the foolish one.

219
00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:52.990
<v Speaker 2>This is the questioner whose disease arises from a profound lack of intelligence and foolishness.

220
00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:58.590
<v Speaker 1>And the master uses an extraordinary reference here involving Jesus, peace be upon him.

221
00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:00.030
<v Speaker 1>How does he frame this?

222
00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:11.630
<v Speaker 2>He notes that Jesus, peace be upon him, stated that while he was, by the absolute permission of God, capable of restoring the dead to life, he was utterly incapable of curing the fool and the ignorant.

223
00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:17.310
<v Speaker 1>To have the miraculous capacity to raise the dead, but to find foolishness an insurmountable barrier.

224
00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:20.190
<v Speaker 2>It really highlights the absolute stubbornness of this condition.

225
00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:23.950
<v Speaker 1>How exactly does the master define this particular brand of foolishness?

226
00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:26.270
<v Speaker 1>It cannot just be a lack of basic education.

227
00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:28.470
<v Speaker 2>No, it is a very specific arrogance.

228
00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:29.310
<v Speaker 2>ignorance.

229
00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:35.430
<v Speaker 2>He defines the fool as someone who has spent only a brief, superficial fraction of time acquiring knowledge.

230
00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:36.470
<v Speaker 1>Just scratch the surface.

231
00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:37.470
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

232
00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:42.030
<v Speaker 2>They have barely touched intellectual and transmitted religious learning.

233
00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:54.590
<v Speaker 2>And yet, operating from that position of profound lack, they raise aggressive, sweeping objections against scholars who have devoted their entire decades long lives to mastering those very disciplines.

234
00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:58.390
<v Speaker 1>It is the ultimate manifestation of not knowing what you do not know.

235
00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:04.830
<v Speaker 1>the arrogance of assuming that a surface level reading equates to a lifetime of deep synthesis.

236
00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:10.270
<v Speaker 2>The master is pointing out an epistemological gap that simply cannot be bridged by a quick conversation.

237
00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:11.310
<v Speaker 2>The gap is too wide.

238
00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:21.950
<v Speaker 2>The vast architecture of insight, the layered context, the historical nuance, and the lived experience possessed by a lifelong scholar are entirely beyond the comprehension of this foolish questioner.

239
00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:22.830
<v Speaker 2>They do not even have.

240
00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.590
<v Speaker 1>The tools to understand the answer.

241
00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:27.230
<v Speaker 2>They lack the vocabulary and the conceptual framework.

242
00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:27.910
<v Speaker 2>They're very

243
00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:31.830
<v Speaker 2>Every incomprehension forms the wall of their foolishness.

244
00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:37.390
<v Speaker 1>So if the gap is that vast, what is the instruction for the disciple when confronted by the foolish one?

245
00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:38.750
<v Speaker 1>Is it the same as the jealous one?

246
00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:39.950
<v Speaker 2>It is exactly the same.

247
00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:40.430
<v Speaker 2>Keep away.

248
00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:41.310
<v Speaker 2>Offer no response.

249
00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:42.430
<v Speaker 1>Offer no response.

250
00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:48.750
<v Speaker 2>You cannot pour an ocean into a thimble, and if you try, you will only damage the thimble and spill the water.

251
00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:49.390
<v Speaker 1>I like that.

252
00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:58.110
<v Speaker 2>Any attempt to explain the nuances of a lifelong study to someone who fundamentally lacks the capacity to grasp the basics

253
00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:03.070
<v Speaker 2>and them twisting your words, they will walk away more entrenched in their ignorance.

254
00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:06.030
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to the third category of patient.

255
00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:09.710
<v Speaker 1>We have the jealous, we have the foolish, who is the third?

256
00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.750
<v Speaker 2>The third patient is the impatient, simple-minded one.

257
00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:14.590
<v Speaker 1>The impatient, simple-minded one.

258
00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:20.870
<v Speaker 2>This is a person who is not necessarily malicious like the jealous one or aggressively arrogant like the foolish one.

259
00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:22.510
<v Speaker 1>Okay, so a different flavor of obstacle.

260
00:15:22.510 --> 00:15:22.910
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

261
00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:31.870
<v Speaker 2>Because of their restlessness, impatience, and immersion in the material world, they simply cannot absorb the teachings of the spiritually elevated.

262
00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:32.990
<v Speaker 1>They are too distracted.

263
00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:41.230
<v Speaker 2>They rely entirely on their own deficient understanding, and they interpret everything you say only through the filter of their own personal worldly interests.

264
00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:43.070
<v Speaker 1>I think we encounter this constantly.

265
00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:46.750
<v Speaker 1>This is the person who hears a profound spiritual truth.

266
00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:51.310
<v Speaker 1>but immediately tries to map it onto their own limited desires.

267
00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:52.190
<v Speaker 2>Constantly.

268
00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:59.630
<v Speaker 1>They run everything through a filter of how does this help my career or how does this validate my current lifestyle.

269
00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:03.310
<v Speaker 2>Yes, they reduce the transcendent to the transactional.

270
00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:04.190
<v Speaker 1>The transactional.

271
00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:12.590
<v Speaker 2>Their intellect is currently incapable of grasping higher spiritual realities because it is weighed down by base attachments.

272
00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:14.590
<v Speaker 1>So what does the master advise for them?

273
00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:17.310
<v Speaker 2>For this person too, the master says there is

274
00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:21.070
<v Speaker 2>no answer you can force upon them that will elevate them in that moment.

275
00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:22.430
<v Speaker 1>You cannot force the medicine.

276
00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:25.550
<v Speaker 2>He grounds this firmly in a known prophetic command.

277
00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:30.110
<v Speaker 2>We are instructed to speak to people strictly according to the capacity of their minds.

278
00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:33.150
<v Speaker 1>You have to meet them where they actually are, not where you want them to be.

279
00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.870
<v Speaker 2>You must respect the limits of their current capacity.

280
00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:42.110
<v Speaker 2>A physician cannot prescribe a heavy, complex regimen to a patient whose body is too weak to handle it.

281
00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:42.910
<v Speaker 1>Would crush them.

282
00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:53.150
<v Speaker 2>If you try to force high-level spiritual realities onto a mind that is entirely consumed by worldly interests, you will end up harming both yourself and the listener.

283
00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:54.750
<v Speaker 1>How does it harm the listener?

284
00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:59.550
<v Speaker 2>The listener will misinterpret the teaching perhaps in ways that are spiritually dangerous to them.

285
00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:03.790
<v Speaker 2>And the teacher will have failed in their basic duty of care.

286
00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:06.110
<v Speaker 1>So we have 3 distinct categories of questioners.

287
00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:10.190
<v Speaker 1>The jealous one, the foolish one, and the impatient simple-minded one.

288
00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:10.590
<v Speaker 2>Right.

289
00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:15.550
<v Speaker 1>And the instruction for all three is essentially to recognize the limits of the medicine.

290
00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:19.870
<v Speaker 1>Do not force a cure where the conditions for healing do not exist.

291
00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:20.430
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

292
00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:21.990
<v Speaker 1>That leaves only the 4th category.

293
00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:24.350
<v Speaker 2>The 4th patient is the genuine seeker.

294
00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:25.310
<v Speaker 1>The genuine seeker.

295
00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:28.750
<v Speaker 2>This is the individual who is actually seeking the right path of guidance.

296
00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:29.950
<v Speaker 2>They are obedient.

297
00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:38.030
<v Speaker 2>They possess a sharp and intelligent mind capable of deep comprehension, and crucially, they are free from the internal static of anger.

298
00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:39.310
<v Speaker 1>Free from anger.

299
00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:48.110
<v Speaker 2>They are free from following the base desires of the lower self, free from the rot of jealousy, and free from the blinding craving for wealth and fame.

300
00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:52.990
<v Speaker 1>They have done the hard work of clearing the internal debris that usually blocks understanding.

301
00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:54.190
<v Speaker 2>Their slate is clean.

302
00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:55.550
<v Speaker 1>So when they ask a question.

303
00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:59.790
<v Speaker 2>When this person asks a question, or even when they raise a rigorous objection,

304
00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:01.950
<v Speaker 2>from a desire to find fault.

305
00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:02.750
<v Speaker 2>It is not a trap.

306
00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:07.150
<v Speaker 2>It doesn't come from a need to test the teacher or show off to an audience.

307
00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:12.670
<v Speaker 2>It arises from an earnest, desperate search for truth and correct guidance.

308
00:18:13.150 --> 00:18:16.110
<v Speaker 1>And I imagine the master's ruling on how to handle this person.

309
00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:18.670
<v Speaker 1>is entirely different.

310
00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:20.030
<v Speaker 2>It is the exact opposite.

311
00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:22.750
<v Speaker 2>This is the patient who can actually be cured.

312
00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:23.550
<v Speaker 1>The only one.

313
00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:29.870
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, to answer the questions of this genuine seeker is not merely permissible for the disciple.

314
00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.230
<v Speaker 2>It is a religiously required obligation.

315
00:18:33.230 --> 00:18:34.030
<v Speaker 1>You have to answer.

316
00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:37.790
<v Speaker 2>You have no right to withhold the medicine from a patient who is ready to receive it.

317
00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:41.870
<v Speaker 1>I deeply appreciate how the master frames the boundaries of teaching here.

318
00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:46.750
<v Speaker 1>The inability to cure the first three patients is not a failure of the teacher's knowledge.

319
00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:47.310
<v Speaker 2>Not at all.

320
00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:49.950
<v Speaker 1>It is simply the reality of the patient's condition.

321
00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:54.110
<v Speaker 1>But when the medicine can work, withholding it becomes a spiritual crime.

322
00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:55.950
<v Speaker 2>Yes, a crime against the seeker.

323
00:18:56.110 --> 00:19:01.390
<v Speaker 1>This entire aside about debate and questioners is really about preserving the purity of knowledge.

324
00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:07.230
<v Speaker 1>It is about not throwing precious insights into the fires of envy or against the walls of ignorance,

325
00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:09.790
<v Speaker 1>but planting them where they can actually take root.

326
00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:11.550
<v Speaker 2>That is a perfect summary.

327
00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:17.230
<v Speaker 2>And understanding that dynamic seamlessly sets up the master's second major admonition.

328
00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:18.270
<v Speaker 1>The second refraining.

329
00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:18.750
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

330
00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:23.870
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple to categorically refrain from the reality of preaching and making speeches.

331
00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:29.310
<v Speaker 1>Now, given that the disciple is a scholar, preaching would seem like the natural outlet for his knowledge.

332
00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:32.590
<v Speaker 1>Why this massive prohibition on delivering sermons?

333
00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:37.710
<v Speaker 2>The grounds for this prohibition are brutally simple, and they are entirely focused on integrity.

334
00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:38.350
<v Speaker 1>Integrity.

335
00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:44.270
<v Speaker 2>A preacher cannot possibly lecture others from a soul that he has not first rigorously instructed himself.

336
00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:47.070
<v Speaker 1>Self-instruction is the absolute prerequisite.

337
00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:49.390
<v Speaker 1>You cannot give what you do not possess.

338
00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:53.630
<v Speaker 2>He drives this home by citing a divine warning given to the son of Mary.

339
00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:54.830
<v Speaker 1>What was the warning?

340
00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:56.590
<v Speaker 2>The command was stark.

341
00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:58.830
<v Speaker 2>First admonish your own soul.

342
00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:06.110
<v Speaker 2>Then only if your own soul accepts your counsel and alters its behavior, you may step forward and admonish others.

343
00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:08.110
<v Speaker 1>And if your soul does not accept it?

344
00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:10.670
<v Speaker 2>Otherwise you should have immense shame before your creator.

345
00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:12.350
<v Speaker 1>Have shame before your creator.

346
00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:21.150
<v Speaker 1>That points to the deep hypocrisy of telling a crowd to abstain from a hidden sin that you yourself indulge in when no one is looking.

347
00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:22.430
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

348
00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:24.150
<v Speaker 1>Or urging them to pursue a virtue.

349
00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:26.830
<v Speaker 1>that you are too lazy to practice in your own home.

350
00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:31.510
<v Speaker 2>It is a spiritual contradiction that slowly destroys the preacher from the inside out.

351
00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:36.670
<v Speaker 2>The gap between public words and private reality becomes a canyon that the soul falls into.

352
00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:37.470
<v Speaker 1>A canyon.

353
00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:40.990
<v Speaker 2>However, the master acknowledges the reality of the disciple's position.

354
00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:51.390
<v Speaker 2>He says that if circumstances arise that absolutely compel the disciple to preach, if the duty falls upon him and cannot be avoided, then there are two massive guards he must establish to protect his soul.

355
00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:54.150
<v Speaker 1>Let us examine these guards closely because

356
00:20:54.230 --> 00:20:55.870
<v Speaker 1>They seem incredibly strict.

357
00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:00.990
<v Speaker 1>Guard 1 deals with the external delivery, what a sermon must not be.

358
00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:01.390
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

359
00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:07.310
<v Speaker 2>The master demands an absolute, uncompromising rejection of the performative pulpit.

360
00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:08.190
<v Speaker 1>No performance.

361
00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:12.110
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple to avoid using colorful, entertaining narrations.

362
00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:15.710
<v Speaker 2>Avoid theatrical gestures designed to captivate the eye.

363
00:21:15.790 --> 00:21:17.550
<v Speaker 1>Nothing designed just for engagement.

364
00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:18.270
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

365
00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:24.510
<v Speaker 2>Do not use secretive, esoteric language that simply makes you sound mystically superior to the common people.

366
00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:29.990
<v Speaker 2>Avoid flowery, embellished speech and do not rely on pleasing poetry to soothe the audience.

367
00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:34.990
<v Speaker 1>He is essentially rejecting every single technique that makes a public speaker popular.

368
00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:36.110
<v Speaker 2>Every single one.

369
00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:39.310
<v Speaker 1>He's stripping away everything that draws a crowd or creates entertainment.

370
00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:39.710
<v Speaker 1>Why?

371
00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:43.150
<v Speaker 2>Because the creator does not love the pretentious and the showy.

372
00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:52.270
<v Speaker 2>The master explains that if a preacher's primary aim is to impress the audience with eloquently crafted language and clever phrasing, then his inner being is already corrupt.

373
00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:54.430
<v Speaker 1>His focus is entirely on himself.

374
00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:58.110
<v Speaker 2>His heart is completely heedless of the genuine remembrance of God.

375
00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:01.310
<v Speaker 2>He is playing a linguistic game while the house is burning down.

376
00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:02.590
<v Speaker 1>And to make the true,

377
00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:08.670
<v Speaker 1>severe purpose of a sermon painfully clear, he introduces an unforgettable analogy.

378
00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:14.430
<v Speaker 2>He asks the disciple to consider a person whose house has been struck by a sudden, violent flood.

379
00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:15.550
<v Speaker 1>A flash flood.

380
00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:18.030
<v Speaker 2>The raging water has reached the very door.

381
00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:23.630
<v Speaker 2>Within seconds, it threatens to engulf the entire structure and drown everyone trapped inside.

382
00:22:23.710 --> 00:22:26.750
<v Speaker 1>It is a moment of absolute paralyzing terror.

383
00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:31.230
<v Speaker 2>In that exact moment of life-ending catastrophe, what does the owner of the house do?

384
00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:32.390
<v Speaker 2>He doesn't craft a beautiful.

385
00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:34.510
<v Speaker 1>He doesn't compose a poem.

386
00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:38.590
<v Speaker 2>He cries out urgently, using the shortest, sharpest warning possible.

387
00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:40.830
<v Speaker 1>L-A-J-Z-R.

388
00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.790
<v Speaker 2>Beware, beware, run, the flood water is here.

389
00:22:43.790 --> 00:22:45.310
<v Speaker 1>He just screams for them to get out.

390
00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:45.950
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

391
00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:54.590
<v Speaker 2>At a moment of imminent disaster, no one in their right mind would describe the rising waters using ornate sentences, secretive vocabulary, or embellished prose.

392
00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:58.670
<v Speaker 1>You wouldn't use decorated poetry to warn someone they're seconds away from drowning.

393
00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:02.310
<v Speaker 2>The master says a sermon must carry this exact same

394
00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:03.590
<v Speaker 2>psychological weight.

395
00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:06.910
<v Speaker 1>The exact gravity of impending disaster.

396
00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:09.470
<v Speaker 1>That completely shifts the purpose of a gathering.

397
00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:19.710
<v Speaker 2>A sermon must be rooted in a genuine, overwhelming fear of one's own reckoning before the creator, and it must convey that exact same unfiltered urgency to the audience.

398
00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:20.750
<v Speaker 1>So it is not a lecture.

399
00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:23.310
<v Speaker 2>A sermon is not an intellectual entertainment.

400
00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:24.830
<v Speaker 2>It is a rescue mission.

401
00:23:24.910 --> 00:23:25.630
<v Speaker 1>A rescue mission.

402
00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:30.590
<v Speaker 2>Its plain, unvarnished purpose is to speak of the severe punishment of the hereafter.

403
00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:32.310
<v Speaker 2>It is designed to bring the audience

404
00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:35.870
<v Speaker 2>face to face with the reality of absolute accountability.

405
00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:38.110
<v Speaker 1>What specifically should they be talking about?

406
00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:42.550
<v Speaker 2>The preacher must describe the awe-inspiring appearance of the Angel of Death.

407
00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:49.470
<v Speaker 2>The terrifying questioning in the grave, the incomprehensible spectacle of accountability on the day of judgment.

408
00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:50.590
<v Speaker 1>The actual stakes.

409
00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:54.830
<v Speaker 2>The precise weighing of deeds, the perilous crossing of the bridge over hell.

410
00:23:54.830 --> 00:24:00.190
<v Speaker 1>He is saying that the existential stakes of human life are far too high for flowery speech.

411
00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:01.230
<v Speaker 2>Way too high.

412
00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:06.510
<v Speaker 1>If you are using poetry to entertain the crowd, you have forgotten that the house is currently flooding.

413
00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:09.390
<v Speaker 1>You are distracting the drowning people with your vocabulary.

414
00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:10.350
<v Speaker 2>That is exactly it.

415
00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:12.830
<v Speaker 2>You are providing them with aesthetic pleasure

416
00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:14.830
<v Speaker 2>when you should be throwing them a lifeline.

417
00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:16.830
<v Speaker 1>That brings us to guard 2.

418
00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:26.430
<v Speaker 1>If guard 1 dictates what the sermon must not be externally, guard 2 is about what the heart must not seek internally.

419
00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:34.910
<v Speaker 2>This addresses the hidden, deeply poisonous motivations that can infect the preacher, even if he uses stark language.

420
00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:36.110
<v Speaker 1>The internal trap.

421
00:24:36.110 --> 00:24:42.830
<v Speaker 2>The master warns that while delivering a sermon, you must aggressively guard your heart against the thought that after you finish,

422
00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:44.750
<v Speaker 2>The people will shout your praises.

423
00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:47.470
<v Speaker 1>The quiet, desperate desire for the crowd's reaction.

424
00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:53.630
<v Speaker 2>The secret hope that the audience will fall into visible ecstasy or tear their clothes in a display of religious fervor.

425
00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:55.790
<v Speaker 1>Or raise a massive clamor in the gathering.

426
00:24:55.790 --> 00:25:02.350
<v Speaker 2>Exactly, declaring to each other what a wonderful, transformative sermon was just given by this uniquely amazing person.

427
00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:06.430
<v Speaker 1>It is the desire to be the focal point of their spiritual experience.

428
00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:12.670
<v Speaker 1>You want them to feel something deeply, but you want to ensure that you are recognized as the one who made them feel it.

429
00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:15.630
<v Speaker 2>Is pure, concentrated ostentation.

430
00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:19.950
<v Speaker 2>It is performing for the creation rather than serving the creator.

431
00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:21.430
<v Speaker 1>And the master is having none of it.

432
00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:30.750
<v Speaker 2>He says that to be pleased by these thoughts or to engineer your sermon to produce these reactions is absolute proof of a catastrophic lack of wisdom.

433
00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:36.270
<v Speaker 2>The pulpit has simply become a stage for the preacher's ego to harvest admiration.

434
00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:41.550
<v Speaker 1>And what happens when a sermon or a community is driven by that kind of ostentation?

435
00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:42.910
<v Speaker 1>What is the outcome?

436
00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:46.430
<v Speaker 2>It becomes a source of complete ruin for both the preacher and the audience.

437
00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:47.070
<v Speaker 1>Both of them.

438
00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:50.110
<v Speaker 2>The master is incredibly forceful in his language here.

439
00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:54.830
<v Speaker 2>He says such a person has become a willing slave to his own degraded lower self.

440
00:25:55.150 --> 00:26:01.110
<v Speaker 2>He is imagining that the sacred pulpit is just a theater to display his rhetorical abilities and harvest the

441
00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:02.590
<v Speaker 2>fame of the world.

442
00:26:02.590 --> 00:26:04.430
<v Speaker 1>And what is the master's ruling on this?

443
00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:06.430
<v Speaker 2>He delivers a strict ruling.

444
00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:16.110
<v Speaker 2>He says that whoever has the authority to remove such a performative preacher from the pulpit, it is obligatory upon them to do so deliberately and firmly.

445
00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:17.350
<v Speaker 1>That is a staggering command.

446
00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:24.730
<v Speaker 1>He is saying it is an obligation to silence them because they are actively distorting the entire purpose of religion.

447
00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:29.130
<v Speaker 1>They are weaponizing the fear of God to build a monument to themselves.

448
00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:29.850
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

449
00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:38.170
<v Speaker 2>The true intention of a sermon must be purely to summon God's creation away from their obsession with this world and orient them toward the reality of the next.

450
00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:39.530
<v Speaker 1>It is a reorientation.

451
00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:42.570
<v Speaker 2>It is meant to cultivate piety where there is heedlessness.

452
00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:45.690
<v Speaker 2>and to violently loosen their attachment to material things.

453
00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:52.090
<v Speaker 2>If it fails to do that and instead just feeds the preacher's vanity, it is a spiritual disaster that must be stopped.

454
00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:58.890
<v Speaker 1>Which leads us into the third admonition, and this one feels almost violently countercultural, especially for anyone who understands how influence works.

455
00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:03.450
<v Speaker 1>The master commands the disciple to stay entirely away from rulers.

456
00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:06.890
<v Speaker 2>He delivers an unusually sharp categorical rule.

457
00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:13.850
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple, you should not greet any king, any person of immense wealth, or any ruler.

458
00:27:13.850 --> 00:27:14.890
<v Speaker 1>Total isolation.

459
00:27:14.890 --> 00:27:18.330
<v Speaker 2>You must stay entirely away from their company and their gatherings.

460
00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:21.850
<v Speaker 2>And he pushes the boundary even further to ensure there is no loophole.

461
00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:24.570
<v Speaker 2>He says, do not so much as lift your eyes to look at them.

462
00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:25.770
<v Speaker 1>Do not even look at them.

463
00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:29.770
<v Speaker 1>Why such total uncompromising isolation from the centers of power?

464
00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.490
<v Speaker 2>Because of what proximity does to the soul.

465
00:27:32.650 --> 00:27:36.570
<v Speaker 1>The standard rationalization is always, if I can just get close to the ruler,

466
00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:38.090
<v Speaker 1>just get a seat at the table.

467
00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:39.930
<v Speaker 1>I can influence them for good.

468
00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:42.730
<v Speaker 1>I can provide moral counsel where it is needed most.

469
00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:50.090
<v Speaker 2>The master dismisses that rationalization entirely because he is looking at the unforgiving psychological reality of power dynamics.

470
00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:51.290
<v Speaker 1>What is that reality?

471
00:27:51.290 --> 00:28:02.410
<v Speaker 2>He says that in looking upon rulers and keeping their company lie, many hidden, devastating calamities, if a scholar finds himself in their presence, he will inevitably find himself praising them.

472
00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:04.090
<v Speaker 1>Or at least validating them quietly.

473
00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:06.850
<v Speaker 2>Validating their authority simply by his quiet

474
00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:07.770
<v Speaker 2>in their court.

475
00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:12.650
<v Speaker 2>And the master grounds this severe prohibition in a clear prophetic teaching.

476
00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:17.050
<v Speaker 2>A law is angered when any transgressor or oppressor is praised.

477
00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:22.090
<v Speaker 1>And rulers, simply by the nature of managing vast wealth and coercive power,

478
00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:25.050
<v Speaker 1>frequently tread into transgression.

479
00:28:25.130 --> 00:28:25.530
<v Speaker 2>They do.

480
00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:27.450
<v Speaker 1>They make decisions that crush the weak.

481
00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:30.490
<v Speaker 1>To praise them is to endorse that crushing.

482
00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:34.410
<v Speaker 2>But the psychological logic the master unpacks goes even deeper.

483
00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:37.530
<v Speaker 2>He explains the terrifying sequence of causality.

484
00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:39.290
<v Speaker 1>This is the part that is really striking.

485
00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:47.210
<v Speaker 2>He says that whoever prays for a long life for an oppressor has in effect expressed a desire that sin and oppression be perpetuated upon the earth.

486
00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:51.290
<v Speaker 1>I want to trace the exact steps of that logic because it is incredibly subtle.

487
00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:52.970
<v Speaker 1>If I sit with a ruler and I

488
00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:55.450
<v Speaker 1>begin to enjoy their company, what happens next?

489
00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:59.290
<v Speaker 2>You will inevitably begin to enjoy the favors that come with their company.

490
00:28:59.290 --> 00:28:59.930
<v Speaker 1>The perks.

491
00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:08.370
<v Speaker 2>You will enjoy their wealth, their fine food, the deference you receive from others, and the elevated status they confer upon you simply by allowing you to stand in their court.

492
00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:09.050
<v Speaker 1>It feels good.

493
00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:10.170
<v Speaker 2>It does.

494
00:29:10.490 --> 00:29:17.610
<v Speaker 2>And because human nature is wired to love the source of its comfort, you will naturally begin to love the one providing those favors.

495
00:29:18.170 --> 00:29:21.770
<v Speaker 2>you will feel a genuine, growing affection for the oppressor.

496
00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:31.050
<v Speaker 1>And if I feel affection for them and rely on their favors, I will naturally pray that they live a long time and remain in power so that I can continue to benefit from them.

497
00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:32.090
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

498
00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:36.090
<v Speaker 2>And in praying for their long life and the continuation of their rule,

499
00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:40.330
<v Speaker 2>You are inherently praying for the prolongation of their oppression over the week.

500
00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:48.570
<v Speaker 1>So your own personal desire for comfort and status has quietly manipulated your heart into actively wishing for the continued suffering of others.

501
00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:49.130
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

502
00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:52.970
<v Speaker 2>The piety of the disciple simply cannot afford that kind of affection.

503
00:29:53.290 --> 00:29:58.970
<v Speaker 2>The slow, invisible infection of proximity to power is almost impossible for the human heart to resist.

504
00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:02.490
<v Speaker 1>That psychological progression is terrifyingly realistic.

505
00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:04.890
<v Speaker 1>You do not set out to support oppression.

506
00:30:05.050 --> 00:30:07.370
<v Speaker 1>You do not wake up deciding to endorse tyranny.

507
00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:08.130
<v Speaker 2>No one does.

508
00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:12.090
<v Speaker 1>You just set out to have a nice dinner at the palace or to secure a little bit of funding on your project.

509
00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:19.690
<v Speaker 2>And before you even realize it, you are making complex theological excuses for a tyrant just to protect your access to the dinner table.

510
00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:26.250
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us seamlessly to the 4th admonition, the final of the four refrainings, refusing gifts from rulers.

511
00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:31.290
<v Speaker 2>It feels like a direct extension of the third point, but he elevates it to a distinct rule.

512
00:30:31.450 --> 00:30:33.010
<v Speaker 1>It is distinct because the trap

513
00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:36.170
<v Speaker 1>of wealth is so specific and so universally blinding.

514
00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:36.810
<v Speaker 2>It is.

515
00:30:36.890 --> 00:30:38.890
<v Speaker 2>The master states the rule clearly.

516
00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:47.130
<v Speaker 2>You should not accept any gifts or wealth from rulers, even if you are absolutely certain that what they're offering comes from entirely lawful sources.

517
00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:49.130
<v Speaker 1>Even if the money is perfectly clean,

518
00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:50.010
<v Speaker 1>Why?

519
00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:55.210
<v Speaker 1>If the wealth wasn't stolen and it isn't forbidden, why can't the scholar take it?

520
00:30:55.370 --> 00:30:58.570
<v Speaker 2>Because taking it corrupts the internal religious path.

521
00:30:58.970 --> 00:31:03.250
<v Speaker 2>It triggers that exact same corruption mechanism we just discussed regarding affection.

522
00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:04.250
<v Speaker 1>The gratitude trap.

523
00:31:04.330 --> 00:31:12.250
<v Speaker 2>Harboring any desire for their possessions and reaching your hand out to take what is theirs causes immediate severe damage to your own path to God.

524
00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:13.690
<v Speaker 1>What is the minimum damage?

525
00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:19.010
<v Speaker 2>The minimum corruption, the very least that will happen to you is that you will begin to love the oppressor who gave you the gift.

526
00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:22.090
<v Speaker 2>Your objectivity will be destroyed by gratitude.

527
00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:25.930
<v Speaker 1>The subtle affection that breeds tolerance for injustice.

528
00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:30.650
<v Speaker 1>You cannot speak truth to power if power is paying your salary.

529
00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:31.130
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

530
00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:34.250
<v Speaker 2>But the master knows his disciple intimately.

531
00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:37.050
<v Speaker 2>He knows the complex architecture of the human mind.

532
00:31:37.130 --> 00:31:38.970
<v Speaker 1>He knows the loopholes we look for.

533
00:31:39.050 --> 00:31:45.770
<v Speaker 2>He knows exactly how a highly educated, deeply religious person will try to rationalize taking the money.

534
00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:50.010
<v Speaker 1>We are infinitely creative when it comes to rationalizing what we want to do anyway.

535
00:31:50.010 --> 00:31:50.490
<v Speaker 2>We are.

536
00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:54.650
<v Speaker 2>The disciple or any listener grappling with this text might immediately think,

537
00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:56.730
<v Speaker 2>What if I take the ruler's money?

538
00:31:56.730 --> 00:31:59.770
<v Speaker 2>Absolutely not for myself, but just to give it to the poor.

539
00:32:00.010 --> 00:32:01.530
<v Speaker 1>I'm strong enough to resist the wealth.

540
00:32:01.690 --> 00:32:07.170
<v Speaker 2>I will take this vast sum and distribute it, bringing much needed comfort to the needy and the marginalized.

541
00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:10.730
<v Speaker 1>It sounds like a deeply noble, entirely selfless intention.

542
00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:12.970
<v Speaker 1>You are acting as a conduit for good.

543
00:32:13.130 --> 00:32:17.850
<v Speaker 2>The master exposes this exact pious sounding thought as a lethal trap.

544
00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:20.410
<v Speaker 2>He says, beware, beware.

545
00:32:20.410 --> 00:32:21.370
<v Speaker 1>It is the bait.

546
00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.490
<v Speaker 2>This specific rationalization is the lure.

547
00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:26.890
<v Speaker 2>It is not your own noble idea.

548
00:32:27.130 --> 00:32:35.530
<v Speaker 2>It is bait set directly by Satan, who uses both invisible forces and the whispers of other human beings to plant this justification in your heart.

549
00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:41.210
<v Speaker 1>Uses the idea of charity, the idea of helping the poor, to corrupt the scholar.

550
00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:44.810
<v Speaker 1>That is a brilliant, terrifying manipulation.

551
00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:47.050
<v Speaker 2>Because Aiten understands leverage.

552
00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:52.810
<v Speaker 2>He knows that once the scholar's hand reaches out and takes the gold, the scholar is permanently compromised.

553
00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:54.330
<v Speaker 1>The trap snaps shut.

554
00:32:54.490 --> 00:33:00.170
<v Speaker 2>The scholar effectively becomes an employee of the state, quietly dependent on the ruler's ongoing favor.

555
00:33:00.970 --> 00:33:03.370
<v Speaker 2>The poor, whom the scholar claimed to be helping,

556
00:33:03.810 --> 00:33:08.890
<v Speaker 2>become nothing more than a moral shield used to justify the scholar's own initial compromise.

557
00:33:08.970 --> 00:33:12.090
<v Speaker 1>So the poor are just an excuse for the scholar's lack of discipline.

558
00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:19.930
<v Speaker 2>The master warns that Satan has shed the blood of many and ruined the faith of countless scholars through this very strategy of noble-sounding rationalization.

559
00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:26.970
<v Speaker 1>You justify your proximity to power by claiming you are doing it for the marginalized, but in reality you have just sold your independence.

560
00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:31.130
<v Speaker 2>It is a devastating critique of institutional religious compromise.

561
00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:33.210
<v Speaker 2>For those who want to explore the sheer

562
00:33:33.650 --> 00:33:40.010
<v Speaker 2>of the calamities caused by court money and proximity to power, the master refers the disciple to his larger work.

563
00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:40.810
<v Speaker 1>Where does he point him?

564
00:33:40.890 --> 00:33:48.170
<v Speaker 2>He points him to the master's larger work, the revival of religious knowledge, where he details exactly how wealth dismantles piety.

565
00:33:48.170 --> 00:33:50.810
<v Speaker 1>So we have covered the four things to refuse.

566
00:33:51.370 --> 00:33:54.330
<v Speaker 1>The outward, seemingly respectable public traps,

567
00:33:54.810 --> 00:34:05.210
<v Speaker 1>refusing the ego feed of debate, refusing the theater of preaching without inward transformation, refusing the proximity to rulers, and refusing the corrupting gravity of their wealth.

568
00:34:05.290 --> 00:34:06.410
<v Speaker 2>The 4 refrainings.

569
00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:09.290
<v Speaker 1>This marks a profound shift in the architecture of the letter.

570
00:34:09.930 --> 00:34:17.210
<v Speaker 1>We are transitioning away from the public dangers into the quiet, private disciplines, the things the disciple must actively do.

571
00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:25.770
<v Speaker 2>Of these eight admonitions, there are four things you must do and four things you must not do so that your knowledge does not turn against you on the day of judgment.

572
00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:26.970
<v Speaker 1>The spine returns.

573
00:34:27.370 --> 00:34:31.490
<v Speaker 1>It grounds us again in the reality that knowledge must become conduct.

574
00:34:32.090 --> 00:34:36.730
<v Speaker 1>So we leave the noise of the public square and we enter the silent, private chambers of the heart.

575
00:34:37.770 --> 00:34:39.370
<v Speaker 1>What is the first taking up?

576
00:34:39.530 --> 00:34:43.770
<v Speaker 2>The first taking up is the method of developing an intimate relationship with the law.

577
00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:50.090
<v Speaker 2>And the master provides an auditing mechanism for this relationship that is piercing in its clarity.

578
00:34:50.090 --> 00:34:51.050
<v Speaker 1>What is the mechanism?

579
00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:54.730
<v Speaker 2>He uses the highly relatable analogy of a household servant.

580
00:34:54.890 --> 00:34:56.330
<v Speaker 1>How exactly does he frame it?

581
00:34:56.410 --> 00:35:07.650
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple that every single matter, every private thought and every interaction between you and your creator should be conducted in such a way that if your own hired servant were to do the exact

582
00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:09.930
<v Speaker 2>exact same thing for you, would not be saddened.

583
00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:10.890
<v Speaker 1>You would be satisfied.

584
00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:14.570
<v Speaker 2>You would approve of it, commend it, and feel absolutely no anger toward them.

585
00:35:14.890 --> 00:35:20.170
<v Speaker 1>I need to audit my relationship with God by looking at the standard I demand from the people who work for me.

586
00:35:20.410 --> 00:35:21.450
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

587
00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:34.570
<v Speaker 2>Conversely, whatever behavior you would deeply dislike your servant doing, whether it is negligence, laziness, doing a job halfway, speaking poorly of you behind your back, or ignoring your clear commands,

588
00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:43.130
<v Speaker 2>You must realize that if you fall short in those exact same ways in your own servitude to your Lord, your creator will equally dislike it.

589
00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:47.850
<v Speaker 1>The precision of this metaphor is deeply uncomfortable because it is so observable.

590
00:35:47.850 --> 00:35:48.730
<v Speaker 2>It is undeniable.

591
00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:57.610
<v Speaker 1>If I ask someone to complete a task and they do it resentfully, or they only do it when I'm physically watching them, or they constantly complain about the effort,

592
00:35:58.090 --> 00:36:00.650
<v Speaker 1>I would be entirely dissatisfied with their service.

593
00:36:00.650 --> 00:36:01.770
<v Speaker 1>I would likely fire them.

594
00:36:01.850 --> 00:36:05.770
<v Speaker 2>You would be angry and rightly so because they violated the terms of the agreement.

595
00:36:05.770 --> 00:36:06.050
<v Speaker 1>Yeah.

596
00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:12.050
<v Speaker 2>And the master drives home the profound asymmetry of this analogy to make sure the disciple feels the weight of it.

597
00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:14.090
<v Speaker 1>The asymmetry between the two relationships.

598
00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:18.690
<v Speaker 2>He says to the disciple, you must realize that your servant was not created by you.

599
00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:19.770
<v Speaker 2>merely employed him.

600
00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:21.770
<v Speaker 2>You pay him a temporary wage.

601
00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:23.050
<v Speaker 1>Transactional arrangement.

602
00:36:23.210 --> 00:36:27.570
<v Speaker 2>But you yourself were created, nurtured, sustained, and are

603
00:36:27.610 --> 00:36:31.530
<v Speaker 2>entirely owned by your real creator and Lord.

604
00:36:31.690 --> 00:36:33.850
<v Speaker 1>The power dynamic is infinitely faster.

605
00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:36.650
<v Speaker 1>I didn't breathe life into my employee.

606
00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:37.690
<v Speaker 1>I just paid him.

607
00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:42.410
<v Speaker 2>You are his slave in the fullest, most absolute existential sense.

608
00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:51.330
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, your obligation not to displease your Lord in thought, word, or deed is immeasurably greater than a hired servant's obligation to you.

609
00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:54.890
<v Speaker 1>So the household metaphor asks a brutally simple question.

610
00:36:54.970 --> 00:37:04.410
<v Speaker 2>If you would resent a certain lack of effort or respect in your servant, how can you possibly justify presenting that exact same lack of effort to the one who breathes life into you?

611
00:37:04.810 --> 00:37:12.170
<v Speaker 1>It strips away all of our grand, complex theological excuses and brings faith down to the most basic, observable standard of human fairness.

612
00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:13.450
<v Speaker 2>It makes it impossible to hide.

613
00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:16.410
<v Speaker 1>If it isn't good enough for me, how can I offer to the divine?

614
00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:18.650
<v Speaker 1>Which moves us beautifully to the second taking up.

615
00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:25.130
<v Speaker 1>If the first discipline was inward and vertical, managing the relationship with the creator, this one rotates outward and horizontal.

616
00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:28.650
<v Speaker 2>The second taking up is the method of right relating to the slaves of Allah.

617
00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:30.010
<v Speaker 1>Relating to humanity.

618
00:37:30.250 --> 00:37:43.370
<v Speaker 2>The master instructs that every matter, every transaction, and every judgment between you and other human beings should be conducted in such a way that if they were to do the exact same thing to you, would be pleased and not aggrieved.

619
00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:45.290
<v Speaker 1>This is the golden rule, essentially.

620
00:37:45.530 --> 00:37:47.290
<v Speaker 1>Treat others as you want to be treated.

621
00:37:47.530 --> 00:37:48.570
<v Speaker 2>Yes, but he

622
00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:51.450
<v Speaker 2>elevates it beyond mere social reciprocity.

623
00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:56.090
<v Speaker 2>He grounds it firmly in a sacred hadith, a tradition where the creator speaks.

624
00:37:56.490 --> 00:37:57.610
<v Speaker 1>What does the tradition say?

625
00:37:57.690 --> 00:38:04.730
<v Speaker 2>The tradition says, my slave's faith is never complete until he loves for all human beings what he loves for himself.

626
00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:08.490
<v Speaker 1>Until he loves for all human beings what he loves for himself.

627
00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:12.970
<v Speaker 1>I want to pause on the sheer impossibility of that standard for the human ego.

628
00:38:13.210 --> 00:38:14.530
<v Speaker 2>It is an immense standard.

629
00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:16.090
<v Speaker 1>That isn't just about acting politely.

630
00:38:16.090 --> 00:38:20.890
<v Speaker 1>That requires an internal emotional parody that feels almost superhuman.

631
00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:24.570
<v Speaker 2>It requires the absolute violent dismantling of selfishness.

632
00:38:25.010 --> 00:38:33.450
<v Speaker 2>It is the exact same auditing instrument used in the first discipline, testing your actions by reversing the roles, but now turned outward toward humanity.

633
00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:34.650
<v Speaker 1>Reversing the roles again.

634
00:38:34.810 --> 00:38:42.810
<v Speaker 2>You cannot claim to possess complete faith if you hoard comfort, security, and wealth for yourself while ignoring the suffering of your neighbor.

635
00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:43.610
<v Speaker 1>You just cannot.

636
00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:53.770
<v Speaker 2>You cannot claim faith if you desperately desire mercy and understanding for your own mistakes, but instantly demand harsh, unforgiving justice for the mistakes of others.

637
00:38:54.770 --> 00:38:56.330
<v Speaker 1>Absolute parody of empathy.

638
00:38:56.730 --> 00:39:02.370
<v Speaker 1>If I want success for my children, I must genuinely want success for my competitors' children.

639
00:39:02.970 --> 00:39:09.530
<v Speaker 1>If I want my hidden flaws to be overlooked, I must actively overlook the hidden flaws of the people who annoy me.

640
00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:13.370
<v Speaker 1>The faith is incomplete until that emotional parody exists.

641
00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:14.410
<v Speaker 2>That is the core of it.

642
00:39:14.650 --> 00:39:19.770
<v Speaker 1>This brings us to the third taking up, which focuses on the disciples' primary daily occupation.

643
00:39:21.370 --> 00:39:27.930
<v Speaker 2>The master offers an insistent advice to study and learn, but he frames the pursuit of knowledge within the ultimate deadline.

644
00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:28.810
<v Speaker 1>The ultimate deadline.

645
00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:31.330
<v Speaker 2>He presents a ruthless, terrifying thought experiment.

646
00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:39.690
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple, if you truly want to increase your knowledge and study scholarly works, imagine that your lifetime will end in no more than a week.

647
00:39:39.770 --> 00:39:40.970
<v Speaker 1>One week left to live.

648
00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:45.050
<v Speaker 2>Consider carefully in that incredibly compressed time frame.

649
00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:51.530
<v Speaker 2>What kind of knowledge will truly benefit you and devote yourself entirely and exclusively to that?

650
00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:55.610
<v Speaker 1>This thought experiment instantly clears the field of everything trivial.

651
00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:56.410
<v Speaker 2>Immediately.

652
00:39:56.410 --> 00:40:02.810
<v Speaker 1>If I know with absolute certainty that I am going to die next Friday, my reading list changes completely.

653
00:40:03.050 --> 00:40:05.850
<v Speaker 1>The things I thought were so urgent suddenly seem ridiculous.

654
00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:08.050
<v Speaker 2>It evaporates everything superficial.

655
00:40:08.050 --> 00:40:11.210
<v Speaker 2>The master lists the things you would immediately drop from your hands.

656
00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:12.170
<v Speaker 1>What would you drop?

657
00:40:12.250 --> 00:40:20.570
<v Speaker 2>You would never spend those final, precious days studying books on religious debate or linguistics or the intricacies of grammar or poetry.

658
00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:21.610
<v Speaker 1>All of that is gone.

659
00:40:21.690 --> 00:40:31.210
<v Speaker 2>You would instantly recognize that such knowledge offers you absolutely no benefit as you stand on the precipice of eternity about to face the ultimate audit.

660
00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:34.410
<v Speaker 1>So what does the disciple actually study in that final week?

661
00:40:34.930 --> 00:40:37.690
<v Speaker 1>What is the only knowledge worth having when the clock runs out?

662
00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:42.010
<v Speaker 2>The Master says you would devote that entire week to the contemplation of the heart.

663
00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:50.410
<v Speaker 2>You would focus entirely on recognizing the deeply embedded faults within your own self so that you might make amends in whatever brief time remains.

664
00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:52.410
<v Speaker 1>You would be racing against time.

665
00:40:52.410 --> 00:41:00.810
<v Speaker 2>You would rush to purify your heart, to sever your attachments to the world, to adorn your soul with the love of Allah, and to beg for forgiveness.

666
00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:04.290
<v Speaker 1>And the unstated, terrifying implication of the thought experiment

667
00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:10.970
<v Speaker 1>is that we should always be studying as if we only have a week left because none of us actually knows when the week is up.

668
00:41:11.050 --> 00:41:11.690
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

669
00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:15.050
<v Speaker 2>Mortality is the only true lens for prioritization.

670
00:41:15.370 --> 00:41:17.690
<v Speaker 1>He sharpens this with a second image, doesn't he?

671
00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:19.930
<v Speaker 2>Does, an equally visceral one, he says.

672
00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:23.850
<v Speaker 2>Imagine you were told that the king was going to visit your home next week.

673
00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:26.730
<v Speaker 1>A royal visitation to your personal residence.

674
00:41:26.810 --> 00:41:28.090
<v Speaker 2>How would you spend that week?

675
00:41:28.090 --> 00:41:31.450
<v Speaker 2>You would be consumed with a single overwhelming concern.

676
00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:36.570
<v Speaker 2>that the king's gaze should not fall upon anything unseemly, dirty, or broken.

677
00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:38.570
<v Speaker 1>You would be scrubbing every corner.

678
00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:40.090
<v Speaker 2>You would scrub the floors.

679
00:41:40.330 --> 00:41:43.250
<v Speaker 2>You would clean your body, your clothing, the walls of the house.

680
00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:45.210
<v Speaker 2>You would hide away anything shameful.

681
00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:47.530
<v Speaker 2>You would leave absolutely nothing untended.

682
00:41:47.770 --> 00:41:51.930
<v Speaker 1>You would prepare the physical space to be worthy of the presence of the sovereign.

683
00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:54.490
<v Speaker 2>And the master doesn't even need to spate the application.

684
00:41:54.490 --> 00:41:55.930
<v Speaker 2>He just shows the reality.

685
00:41:56.170 --> 00:41:57.850
<v Speaker 2>He quotes a prophetic saying,

686
00:41:58.490 --> 00:42:04.730
<v Speaker 2>Verily, Allah does not look at your faces or your outward actions, but he looks at your hearts and your intentions.

687
00:42:05.450 --> 00:42:08.090
<v Speaker 1>The heart is the home that the true king is visiting.

688
00:42:08.330 --> 00:42:11.930
<v Speaker 2>The gaze of the supreme ruler rests entirely and exclusively on the heart.

689
00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:20.250
<v Speaker 2>So the master is asking the disciple, if you would spend a week scrubbing your physical house for a mortal king whose power is temporary,

690
00:42:20.970 --> 00:42:25.770
<v Speaker 2>Why does your heart, which is the sole focus of the divine gaze, go uncurified?

691
00:42:26.090 --> 00:42:27.890
<v Speaker 1>That is a staggering contrast.

692
00:42:27.890 --> 00:42:35.210
<v Speaker 2>Why are you obsessively studying grammar and poetry when the house of your heart is filled with the filth of arrogance, envy, and ostentation?

693
00:42:35.530 --> 00:42:39.370
<v Speaker 1>It is a profound, devastating reorientation of priorities.

694
00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:46.090
<v Speaker 1>He is telling the scholar that intellectual mastery of the texts is utterly useless if the heart holding those texts is a ruin.

695
00:42:46.290 --> 00:42:47.210
<v Speaker 2>Utterly useless.

696
00:42:47.530 --> 00:42:50.410
<v Speaker 2>And this brings us to the 4th and final taking up, which shifts

697
00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:54.090
<v Speaker 2>the intellectual and spiritual to the deeply physical and practical.

698
00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:56.250
<v Speaker 1>What is the final discipline?

699
00:42:56.410 --> 00:42:59.130
<v Speaker 2>It is about not stockpiling provision.

700
00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:01.210
<v Speaker 1>This touches on our deepest anxieties.

701
00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:06.170
<v Speaker 1>This is about how much of the material world we clutch onto for a sense of security.

702
00:43:06.250 --> 00:43:08.330
<v Speaker 2>The master sets a definitive ceiling.

703
00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:15.130
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple, you should not accumulate more than one year's provision for your family from the goods of this world.

704
00:43:15.690 --> 00:43:17.450
<v Speaker 1>A strict one-year provision limit.

705
00:43:17.890 --> 00:43:27.450
<v Speaker 1>In an environment that constantly tells us we need endless security, generational wealth and decades of reserves just to be safe, a one-year limit sounds terrifyingly precarious.

706
00:43:27.850 --> 00:43:29.930
<v Speaker 1>It feels almost reckless to the modern mind.

707
00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:32.010
<v Speaker 2>It is meant to demand actual trust.

708
00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:37.690
<v Speaker 2>It strips away the illusion that your stockpiled grain is what keeps you alive rather than the sustainer.

709
00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:40.410
<v Speaker 1>But he calibrates this rule carefully, doesn't he?

710
00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:46.090
<v Speaker 2>Does, by looking at the established practice of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

711
00:43:46.410 --> 00:43:56.570
<v Speaker 2>He explains how the Prophet set aside exactly one year's worth of provisions for certain members of his family, offering the prayer, O my Allah, make this provision sufficient for the family of Muhammad.

712
00:43:56.730 --> 00:44:00.250
<v Speaker 1>So the one-year mark is an established prophetic precedent.

713
00:44:00.890 --> 00:44:03.450
<v Speaker 1>It isn't just an arbitrary number the master invented.

714
00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:09.130
<v Speaker 2>It is firmly established, but the master points out a vital psychological nuance in the prophet's action.

715
00:44:09.690 --> 00:44:13.290
<v Speaker 2>The prophet did not set aside provisions for all of his wives uniformly.

716
00:44:13.450 --> 00:44:13.890
<v Speaker 1>He didn't.

717
00:44:14.090 --> 00:44:14.410
<v Speaker 2>No.

718
00:44:14.890 --> 00:44:26.570
<v Speaker 2>He reserved A year's supply only for those whose reliance on God, their inner spiritual certainty was slightly weaker, and genuinely needed that physical, tangible reassurance to feel at peace.

719
00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:29.290
<v Speaker 1>He accommodated their human need for security.

720
00:44:29.690 --> 00:44:33.050
<v Speaker 1>He didn't force a level of asceticism on them that their hearts couldn't handle.

721
00:44:33.050 --> 00:44:34.730
<v Speaker 2>Yes, he met them at their capacity.

722
00:44:34.970 --> 00:44:44.370
<v Speaker 2>But for those among the mothers of the believers whose certainty in Allah was absolute, whose trust was perfect and unshakable, the Prophet did not set aside even a single day's provisions.

723
00:44:44.370 --> 00:44:45.850
<v Speaker 1>Not even a single day's provisions.

724
00:44:45.850 --> 00:44:48.490
<v Speaker 1>They woke up with absolutely nothing stored for tomorrow.

725
00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:53.850
<v Speaker 2>The Master specifically names A Aisha as one of those with perfect reliance.

726
00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:56.570
<v Speaker 1>That level of trust is unimaginable for most of us.

727
00:44:56.730 --> 00:45:00.890
<v Speaker 2>The instruction here is profound because it acknowledges human variance.

728
00:45:01.450 --> 00:45:04.370
<v Speaker 2>It does not flatten everyone to the exact same measure.

729
00:45:04.370 --> 00:45:05.730
<v Speaker 1>Right, the rule isn't flat.

730
00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:14.010
<v Speaker 2>The one-year limit is a ceiling for those who might otherwise hoard wealth indefinitely out of a baseline fear of the future, but it is not a floor.

731
00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:23.450
<v Speaker 2>For those whose hearts are already at complete, undisturbed rest in the care of their creator, stockpiling anything at all is entirely unnecessary.

732
00:45:23.690 --> 00:45:24.370
<v Speaker 1>It is a slight

733
00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:25.850
<v Speaker 1>gliding scale of trust.

734
00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:32.010
<v Speaker 1>The more deeply you trust the unseen provider, the less you feel the desperate need to clutch at the seen provisions.

735
00:45:32.010 --> 00:45:32.810
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

736
00:45:32.810 --> 00:45:36.650
<v Speaker 1>If your heart knows who is feeding you, don't need to hoard the bread.

737
00:45:36.730 --> 00:45:37.690
<v Speaker 2>Beautifully said.

738
00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:42.290
<v Speaker 2>And with that calibration of trust, the master concludes the four things to take up.

739
00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:45.050
<v Speaker 1>So we have walked the complete 8 contours.

740
00:45:45.050 --> 00:45:45.610
<v Speaker 2>We have.

741
00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:47.610
<v Speaker 2>The spiritual diagnosis has been made.

742
00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:51.010
<v Speaker 2>The rule of life is firmly placed in the disciple's hand.

743
00:45:51.010 --> 00:45:53.850
<v Speaker 1>And the disciple had asked for one more thing in his original letter.

744
00:45:54.490 --> 00:46:03.370
<v Speaker 1>He had asked the master for a permanent daily prayer, a supplication he could recite to carry this discipline and this immense framework into his everyday life.

745
00:46:03.530 --> 00:46:08.090
<v Speaker 2>The master begins his response to this final request by first widening the field of view.

746
00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:12.170
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple not to rely on his own invention,

747
00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:19.450
<v Speaker 2>not to craft his own poetic prayers, but to source his prayer life directly from the established canonical traditions.

748
00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:20.490
<v Speaker 1>To go to the source.

749
00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:29.850
<v Speaker 2>He specifically points him to the six most authenticated collections of the prophet's traditions and to the powerful prayers passed down through the family of the prophet.

750
00:46:30.010 --> 00:46:34.890
<v Speaker 1>He is telling him to source his prayer from the deepest, purest wells of the tradition.

751
00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:40.250
<v Speaker 1>rather than relying on his own limited, potentially ego-driven vocabulary.

752
00:46:40.410 --> 00:46:50.970
<v Speaker 2>But within that vast field of tradition, the master carefully selects and hands him one specific, sweeping supplication, instructing him to recite it after every single obligatory prayer.

753
00:46:51.130 --> 00:46:53.050
<v Speaker 1>The structure of the prayer itself is beautiful.

754
00:46:53.050 --> 00:46:56.730
<v Speaker 1>It moves in three distinct blocks, mapping perfectly onto the human experience.

755
00:46:57.370 --> 00:47:01.530
<v Speaker 1>The first block is a profound, expansive request for abundance.

756
00:47:01.610 --> 00:47:07.050
<v Speaker 2>The disciple is taught to stand before his Lord and ask for the absolute fullness and perfection of blessings.

757
00:47:07.210 --> 00:47:09.210
<v Speaker 1>What specifically is he asking for?

758
00:47:09.290 --> 00:47:12.890
<v Speaker 2>He asks for the permanence of both inward and outward purity.

759
00:47:13.370 --> 00:47:22.810
<v Speaker 2>He asks for the all-encompassing breadth of mercy, for physical health, for a lawful increase in sustenance, for happiness, and for lasting ultimate success.

760
00:47:22.890 --> 00:47:26.650
<v Speaker 1>He asks for the best of all deeds and the most beneficial of all knowledge.

761
00:47:27.290 --> 00:47:31.810
<v Speaker 1>It is a sweeping unhesitating request for God to pour out his generosity.

762
00:47:31.810 --> 00:47:33.610
<v Speaker 2>It is completely expansive.

763
00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:40.410
<v Speaker 1>But then, having asked for everything good, the prayer shifts into a second block, which is deeply focused on our own flaws.

764
00:47:40.970 --> 00:47:41.530
<v Speaker 1>Correction.

765
00:47:41.530 --> 00:47:45.530
<v Speaker 2>Having asked for divine abundance, the disciple must face his own brokenness.

766
00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:46.810
<v Speaker 2>He prays.

767
00:47:47.530 --> 00:47:49.290
<v Speaker 2>Reform our deeds.

768
00:47:49.290 --> 00:47:52.650
<v Speaker 2>He begs for a veil to be drawn over his sins and his faults.

769
00:47:52.890 --> 00:48:01.130
<v Speaker 2>He asks God to do him the immense favor of actively correcting all his shortcomings, both those hidden deep inside the heart and those visible on the outside for all to see.

770
00:48:01.130 --> 00:48:03.210
<v Speaker 1>It is an admission of ultimate helplessness.

771
00:48:03.530 --> 00:48:05.850
<v Speaker 1>We cannot fix ourselves without divine intervention.

772
00:48:06.170 --> 00:48:07.490
<v Speaker 1>We need him to correct us.

773
00:48:07.490 --> 00:48:08.730
<v Speaker 2>And we need confidence.

774
00:48:08.730 --> 00:48:13.130
<v Speaker 1>Yes, we need confidence and reliance upon him to endure the pain of that correction.

775
00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:16.890
<v Speaker 2>Which flows directly into the third block, deliverance.

776
00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:22.890
<v Speaker 2>The prayer turns away from the immediate world and toward the ultimate stakes of human existence.

777
00:48:23.370 --> 00:48:24.330
<v Speaker 1>Deliverance from what?

778
00:48:24.490 --> 00:48:29.050
<v Speaker 2>The disciple asks for steadfastness and unwavering perseverance on the path to God.

779
00:48:29.930 --> 00:48:37.690
<v Speaker 2>He begs to be saved from committing any deeds in this world that would become a source of shame, exposure, and humiliation on the terrifying day of judgment.

780
00:48:37.930 --> 00:48:40.970
<v Speaker 1>He asks for the crushing burden of his sins to be lightened.

781
00:48:41.050 --> 00:48:47.690
<v Speaker 2>He asks for liberation from the fire of hell, liberation from crippling debt, and liberation from the oppression of others.

782
00:48:47.770 --> 00:48:51.690
<v Speaker 1>The entire structure of the prayer mirrors the structure of the Master's letter.

783
00:48:52.010 --> 00:48:54.490
<v Speaker 1>Abundance, correction, deliverance.

784
00:48:54.490 --> 00:48:55.770
<v Speaker 2>It is a perfect mirror.

785
00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:59.810
<v Speaker 1>And then at the very climax of this supplication, the Master

786
00:48:59.930 --> 00:49:01.610
<v Speaker 1>distinct divine names.

787
00:49:02.250 --> 00:49:07.130
<v Speaker 1>He names the faces of mercy, invoking the Creator by his ultimate defining attributes.

788
00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:09.730
<v Speaker 2>These names carry the weight of the entire universe.

789
00:49:09.730 --> 00:49:10.210
<v Speaker 2>And there is a.

790
00:49:10.210 --> 00:49:12.970
<v Speaker 1>Profound psychological mechanism behind each one.

791
00:49:13.370 --> 00:49:16.490
<v Speaker 1>Why does the soul need these specific names at the end of this journey?

792
00:49:16.730 --> 00:49:21.210
<v Speaker 2>Because each name answers a specific vulnerability of the human condition we just discussed.

793
00:49:21.610 --> 00:49:24.650
<v Speaker 2>He prays, Ya A Zi E.

794
00:49:25.810 --> 00:49:32.690
<v Speaker 2>He calls upon the victorious, the source of all strength, because the disciple is weak and cannot implement these eight contours alone.

795
00:49:32.690 --> 00:49:33.610
<v Speaker 1>He needs strength.

796
00:49:33.770 --> 00:49:35.210
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, Gaff Erar.

797
00:49:35.450 --> 00:49:41.370
<v Speaker 2>He calls upon the endlessly forgiving, because the disciple knows he will inevitably fail and must constantly return.

798
00:49:41.370 --> 00:49:42.970
<v Speaker 1>The cycle of failure in return.

799
00:49:42.970 --> 00:49:48.410
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, Karim, the generous, whose giving is boundless and not limited by our meager merit.

800
00:49:48.410 --> 00:49:50.970
<v Speaker 1>His generosity overrides our inadequacy.

801
00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:52.170
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, Sattar.

802
00:49:52.850 --> 00:49:59.170
<v Speaker 2>The coverer of false, answering the disciples' deep anxiety about having his hidden hypocrisy exposed on the day of judgment.

803
00:49:59.170 --> 00:50:01.290
<v Speaker 1>The fear of exposure is so real.

804
00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:02.090
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, Halim.

805
00:50:02.410 --> 00:50:07.130
<v Speaker 2>The forbearing and compassionate, pleading for God's patience with our slow, stubborn progress.

806
00:50:07.130 --> 00:50:08.490
<v Speaker 1>We are so stubborn.

807
00:50:08.490 --> 00:50:09.770
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, Jabir.

808
00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:15.130
<v Speaker 2>The compeller and the mender, asking God to forcefully fix and restore the shattered pieces of the heart.

809
00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:16.890
<v Speaker 1>The mender of broken things.

